Alpha instruction tests
=======================

Self-checking tests of the Alpha user-mode instruction set, as the 21064
(EV4) implements it. Each test program executes many instructions with
operands from a table and prints the results in hex; the output is compared
line by line with reference output in expected/.

Files:

  gen_tests.py      generates the test programs (NAME.S) and a description
                    of every result line (NAME.list)
  fpmodel.py        exact reference model for the floating point tests
  rt_gxemul.S       runtime for gxemul's "testalpha" machine
  rt_linux.S        runtime for Linux user mode (to get reference output
                    from QEMU)
  common.S          prints the results
  build_tests.sh    assembles and links everything
  run_tests.py      runs the tests in gxemul and compares
  make_expected.py  makes expected/ from QEMU output and fpmodel.py
  expected/         reference output

Running the tests needs an Alpha cross assembler and linker (GNU binutils
for alpha-linux-gnu; on Debian/Ubuntu the binutils-alpha-linux-gnu
package):

  ALPHA_AS=alpha-linux-gnu-as ALPHA_LD=alpha-linux-gnu-ld \
      test/alpha/build_tests.sh /tmp/alphatests
  test/alpha/run_tests.py /tmp/alphatests

Remaking the reference output also needs qemu-alpha (qemu-user):

  for t in $(cat /tmp/alphatests/tests.txt); do
      qemu-alpha -cpu ev4-alpha-cpu /tmp/alphatests/$t.linux \
          > /tmp/alphatests/$t.qemu
  done
  test/alpha/make_expected.py /tmp/alphatests test/alpha/expected

Where the references come from:

  o  Integer and IEEE tests: QEMU's EV4 model. fpmodel.py checks the IEEE
     results too, and its value is used where they disagree (the only
     disagreement is the sign of an underflowed result without /U: QEMU
     keeps the sign, the 21064 gives a true zero, +0; to be confirmed on
     real hardware).
  o  VAX tests: fpmodel.py only. QEMU's VAX floating point is wrong, and
     it has no D_floating (fp_vax crashes in QEMU at the first CVTGD,
     which is expected).

Tests named *_inf use IEEE rounding towards plus or minus infinity, which
the 21064 leaves to software (FEN exception); they run on a 21164.

Only cases that cannot trap are tested in the generated tests. Trapping
behaviour (overflow, invalid operands, /SUI inexact, unaligned accesses,
reserved opcodes, FEN) and the 21064 privileged architecture (PALmode,
IPRs, translation buffers, interrupts, machine checks) are tested by
pal_ev4.S, which is its own small PALcode and runs on the "testalphapal"
machine. Its result lines are described in pal_ev4.list; the expected
values were worked out by hand from the 21064 Hardware Reference Manual.
build_tests.sh runs it through cpp (set CPP to use another one).
